{"review_id":"00001788308111360583-0004098830-000003-cycle-review-1","reviewed_manifest_hash":"sha256:1065cb63075fef1ed70ac3e8cbe75a8c12b8bef1910f0ca30162741b252ef21f","findings":[{"finding_id":"xai-sse-utf8-chunk-boundary-corruption","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"high","blocking":false,"title":"SSE buffer decodes each transport chunk with from_utf8_lossy, corrupting any multi-byte character split across a chunk boundary","claim":"`submit` accumulates the SSE stream with `buffer.push_str(&String::from_utf8_lossy(&chunk))`, converting every `reqwest` transport chunk to UTF-8 independently. `response.chunk()` yields bytes at arbitrary network boundaries, not at codepoint or SSE-frame boundaries, so a multi-byte UTF-8 sequence straddling two chunks is decoded as U+FFFD replacement characters on both sides and the original bytes are lost before the line is ever parsed. Because U+FFFD is itself valid UTF-8 and valid inside a JSON string, `serde_json::from_str` still succeeds and the corruption is silent: assistant text deltas and, more seriously, tool-call `arguments` JSON strings are delivered to the loop with mangled content rather than being rejected or repaired. The correct handling is to buffer raw bytes and decode only complete lines (or hold the trailing incomplete sequence for the next chunk). None of the wire tests exercise this: every fixture body in `xai_api.rs`, `tests/xai.rs`, and `tests/xai_adapter.rs` is pure ASCII delivered by wiremock in one piece, so the passing suite provides no evidence against this path.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/xai_api.rs","range":{"start":542,"end":567}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:681f43323dc39e7c7cb777b0ddc39cdf2e882a6475c286035e16ba9f05fadb99","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":34586,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-61","revision":"sha256:17509570806bbdc7395db3096bb30eeddb0313748438b7ca07065f8f6a280c86","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-681f43323dc39e7c7cb777b0ddc39cdf2e882a6475c286035e16ba9f05fadb99-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Buffer the stream as bytes (e.g. a `Vec<u8>`/`BytesMut`), split complete lines on `b'\\n'` from that byte buffer, and decode only each complete line with `str::from_utf8`, leaving any trailing partial sequence in the buffer for the next chunk. Add a fixture that splits a non-ASCII text delta and a non-ASCII tool-call `arguments` value across two transport chunks and asserts the reassembled value is byte-identical to the source.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"xai-per-attempt-cost-and-model-last-write-wins","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"Vendor cost ticks and resolved model identity are adapter-global last-write-wins state, so all but the final attempt's billing evidence is discarded","claim":"`last_cost_usd_ticks` and `last_resolved_model` are single-slot `Mutex` fields on the shared `XaiAdapter` that each `submit` overwrites, with no association back to the `AttemptId` that produced them. In any multi-attempt run — which is the normal agent-loop case — only the last attempt's values survive, and there is no way for a caller to recover the earlier ones. The package's own loop test demonstrates the loss concretely: the two turns report `cost_in_usd_ticks` of 9000 and 1500 respectively, the loop records `outcome.attempts.len() == 2`, and after the run only 1500 is retrievable. The same slot is also shared across concurrent `submit` calls on one adapter instance (`submit` takes `&self` and the trait is used through a shared reference), so with any concurrency a caller can read another attempt's cost. This contradicts the contract's claim that `XaiAdapter` 'captures it losslessly via `last_cost_usd_ticks()`' as the hook a later PRD plumbs into `provider_cost_lexical`: a later stage wiring this into accounting would attribute one attempt's vendor cost to a different attempt, or silently drop the rest.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/xai_api.rs","range":{"start":128,"end":140}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/tests/xai.rs","range":{"start":152,"end":161}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:681f43323dc39e7c7cb777b0ddc39cdf2e882a6475c286035e16ba9f05fadb99","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":34586,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-61","revision":"sha256:17509570806bbdc7395db3096bb30eeddb0313748438b7ca07065f8f6a280c86","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-681f43323dc39e7c7cb777b0ddc39cdf2e882a6475c286035e16ba9f05fadb99-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Return the per-attempt vendor cost and provider-resolved model on the value the call already produces (extend `SubmitOutcome`, or return them alongside it), rather than through adapter-global mutable slots. If the shared-slot accessors are kept for diagnostics, key them by `AttemptId` and document that they are not a billing-evidence source. Add a test asserting that both attempts of a two-turn run expose their own distinct `cost_in_usd_ticks`.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"xai-reconstructed-tool-calls-fabricate-empty-arguments","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"Reconstructed assistant tool_calls entries send a fabricated \"{}\" arguments string, falsifying every multi-turn transcript","claim":"`wire_messages` synthesizes the assistant `tool_calls` message that must precede each `role: \"tool\"` run, but because `MessageContent::ToolResult` does not carry the original call arguments, each reconstructed entry is emitted with a hard-coded `\"arguments\": \"{}\"`. On every second-and-later turn the model is therefore shown a transcript in which it called `apply-edit` (or any other capability) with no arguments, immediately followed by a tool result that only makes sense for the arguments it actually sent. This is not a wire-acceptance requirement — the code comment correctly notes that only matching ids are required — it is fabricated conversation history that misrepresents the model's own prior actions, and it degrades exactly the multi-turn tool loops this adapter exists to run. The unit tests pin the fabrication rather than catching it: `tool_result_run_gets_a_preceding_assistant_tool_calls_message` and its siblings assert only `id`, `type`, and `name`, never `arguments`, and the wiremock matcher `ToolCallPrecedesEachToolResult` in `tests/xai.rs` likewise checks ids only.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/xai_api.rs","range":{"start":259,"end":275}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/tests/xai.rs","range":{"start":107,"end":137}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-workspace-advisory","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:51b849b76d9c99ba53bb8c44cb2e4b0ffac8a8ec82000222b58c9687fbf013de","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":92983,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-61","revision":"sha256:17509570806bbdc7395db3096bb30eeddb0313748438b7ca07065f8f6a280c86","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-51b849b76d9c99ba53bb8c44cb2e4b0ffac8a8ec82000222b58c9687fbf013de-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Carry the issuing call's `arguments` (and name) through the transcript type so the reconstructed assistant `tool_calls` entry reproduces what the model actually sent — either by extending `ToolResultPayload` with the originating arguments, or by having the loop retain the assistant tool-call message itself. Until then, state the fabrication explicitly in the contract document rather than describing the reconstruction as 'what the wire format actually requires', and add a test asserting arguments round-trip.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"xai-price-schedule-only-exists-inside-a-tautological-test","category":"test_gap","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"The Grok price schedule is a test-local literal asserted against itself; no production code or configuration ships or applies it","claim":"`grok_price_schedule_matches_official_xai_pricing_in_nanousd` builds a `PriceScheduleConfig` from inline literals and then asserts those same literals back (`uncached_input == Some(2_000_000_000)` immediately after constructing it with `Some(2_000_000_000)`), so it cannot fail for any reason related to the product. `grok_estimate_covers_reasoning_tokens_not_just_output_tokens` reimplements the pricing formula in a local `category_cost` closure instead of calling the accounting repository it claims to mirror, and its main assertion is a tautology because the reasoning rate is set equal to the output rate on both sides. `cost_in_usd_ticks_conversion_is_documented_and_exact` asserts arithmetic on two integer literals. No non-test file in the change set registers, ships, or consumes an xAI price schedule — the only other appearance is a TOML snippet inside the contract document. The acceptance criterion that Grok price schedules are versioned, dated, sourced from official xAI pricing 'and estimates record them' therefore has no executing implementation behind it, and the passing suite gives no evidence that any xAI worker's estimate would use these rates.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/tests/xai.rs","range":{"start":347,"end":412}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/tests/xai.rs","range":{"start":413,"end":479}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:681f433