## 1. How SSE bodies are set up in wiremock, and the crux finding on multi-chunk delivery

Every existing test in `xai_api.rs` uses `wiremock::ResponseTemplate::new(200).set_body_raw(body, "text/event-stream")`, which buffers the **entire** SSE body as one `Full<Bytes>` HTTP body — wiremock gives you no control over how that buffer gets split into wire-level chunks, and no `Respond` trait impl or chunk-delay mechanism is used anywhere in this codebase (confirmed by grep — zero hits for `impl Respond`, `delay(`, `StreamBody`, `Body::wrap_stream`).

Critically, this fact is already documented **in this codebase** as a comment in `crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/openai_api.rs` (lines 933-963), where a raw `tokio::net::TcpListener` is used instead of wiremock specifically because wiremock can't do partial/chunked delivery:

```rust
// crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/openai_api.rs:933-963
#[tokio::test]
async fn truncated_body_after_status_is_ambiguous_never_a_free_retry() {
    // A raw server (not wiremock, whose `Full<Bytes>` responses cannot
    // express an in-flight cutoff) sends valid status and headers —
    // the provider has accepted, executed, and billed the request —
    // then drops the connection with the declared `Content-Length`
    // unmet. The failure must surface from `response.text()`, not
    // `send()`, and must never be a free retry.
    use tokio::io::{AsyncReadExt, AsyncWriteExt};
    use tokio::net::TcpListener;

    let listener = TcpListener::bind("127.0.0.1:0").await.unwrap();
    let addr = listener.local_addr().unwrap();
    tokio::spawn(async move {
        let (mut socket, _) = listener.accept().await.unwrap();
        let mut buf = [0u8; 4096];
        // Drain the request so the client isn't blocked writing it.
        let _ = socket.read(&mut buf).await;
        socket
            .write_all(
                b"HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\n\
                  Content-Type: text/event-stream\r\n\
                  Content-Length: 999999\r\n\
                  \r\n\
                  data: {\"type\":\"response.completed\"",
            )
            .await
            .unwrap();
        socket.flush().await.unwrap();
        drop(socket);
    });

    let openai = OpenAiResponsesClient::new(
        ApiKey::new("sk-test"),
        OpenAiResponsesConfig {
            base_url: format!("http://{addr}"),
            request_timeout_secs: 5,
            service_tier: None,
        },
    )
    .unwrap();
    ...
```

**This is the pattern to reuse for a true two-chunk delivery test**: bind a raw `TcpListener`, write correct headers, then do two separate `write_all` + `flush` calls with the body split at whatever byte offset you want (e.g. mid-UTF-8-codepoint), instead of dropping the connection. wiremock cannot express this.

Given `set_body_raw` is buffered whole, all existing "fixture bytes" are just plain SSE frame strings built via the `sse_body` helper (see below) — that's the raw material to split manually into two `write_all` calls if you go the TCP-listener route, or simply accept that a single `set_body_raw` mock cannot be relied upon to prove chunk-boundary behavior at all.

## 2. Full existing successful tool-call streaming test

```rust
// crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/xai_api.rs:776-814
#[tokio::test]
async fn tool_call_arrives_whole_in_a_single_chunk() {
    let server = MockServer::start().await;
    let body = sse_body(&[
        r#"{"id":"req_2","model":"grok-4","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"tool_calls":[{"index":0,"id":"call_abc","type":"function","function":{"name":"read-file","arguments":"{\"path\":\"a.txt\"}"}}]},"finish_reason":"tool_calls"}]}"#,
        "[DONE]",
    ]);
    Mock::given(method("POST"))
        .and(path("/chat/completions"))
        .respond_with(ResponseTemplate::new(200).set_body_raw(body, "text/event-stream"))
        .mount(&server)
        .await;

    let adapter = adapter_for(&server);
    let mut collector = Collector(Vec::new());
    let outcome = adapter
        .submit(&base_request("att_1"), &mut collector)
        .await
        .unwrap();

    assert_eq!(outcome.stop_reason, AdapterStopReason::ToolUse);
    // Whole-chunk delivery: the delta and its completion arrive
    // together, never split across separate submit() turns.
    assert_eq!(collector.0.len(), 2);
    assert_eq!(
        collector.0[0],
        StreamEvent::ToolCallDelta {
            call_id: "call_abc".into(),
            capability_id: "read-file".into(),
            arguments_fragment: "{\"path\":\"a.txt\"}".into(),
        }
    );
    assert_eq!(
        collector.0[1],
        StreamEvent::ToolCallComplete {
            call_id: "call_abc".into(),
        }
    );
}
```

## 3. Helper functions (adapter construction, mock server, observer)

```rust
// crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/xai_api.rs:694-732
fn adapter_for(server: &MockServer) -> XaiAdapter {
    std::env::set_var("XAI_TEST_KEY", "sk-test-key");
    XaiAdapter::new(XaiAdapterConfig {
        base_url: server.uri(),
        auth: AuthDescriptor::Env("XAI_TEST_KEY".into()),
        request_timeout_secs: 5,
    })
    .unwrap()
}

fn base_request(attempt: &str) -> SubmitRequest {
    SubmitRequest {
        attempt_id: AttemptId(attempt.into()),
        messages: vec![Message::user("hi")],
        model: "grok-4".into(),
        tools: vec![],
        structured_output: None,
        cache_control: CacheControl::None,
        reasoning_control: None,
        prompt_cache_key: None,
    }
}

struct Collector(Vec<StreamEvent>);
impl StreamObserver for Collector {
    fn on_event(&mut self, event: StreamEvent) {
        self.0.push(event);
    }
}

fn sse_body(lines: &[&str]) -> String {
    let mut body = String::new();
    for line in lines {
        body.push_str("data: ");
        body.push_str(line);
        body.push_str("\n\n");
    }
    body
}
```

Invocation pattern used everywhere: `adapter.submit(&base_request("att_1"), &mut collector).await` returning `Result<SubmitOutcome, AdapterError>` (or the `(outcome, _)` tuple form is only in `openai_api.rs`, not xai — xai's `submit` returns `Result<SubmitOutcome, AdapterError>` directly, per line 495-499 of `xai_api.rs`).

## 4. Imports, `StreamEvent` variants, and non-ASCII byte-splitting guidance

Test-module imports (`xai_api.rs:689-692`):
```rust
use super::*;
use crate::attempt::{CacheControl, ToolDefinition};
use wiremock::matchers::{header, method, path};
use wiremock::{Mock, MockServer, ResponseTemplate};
```
`wiremock = "0.6"` (workspace root `Cargo.toml:58`; lockfile pins `0.6.5`).

`StreamEvent` (defined in `crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/attempt.rs:116-128`):
```rust
pub enum StreamEvent {
    TextDelta(String),
    ToolCallDelta {
        call_id: String,
        capability_id: String,
        arguments_fragment: String,
    },
    ToolCallComplete {
        call_id: String,
    },
    UsageDelta(UsageCategories),
    Stop(AdapterStopReason),
}
```
`AdapterStopReason` (`attempt.rs:174-180`): `EndTurn`, `ToolUse`, `MaxTokens`, `StopSequence`, `ContentFilter`.
`StreamObserver` trait (`attempt.rs:230-232`): `fn on_event(&mut self, event: StreamEvent);`.

All of these, plus `AdapterError`, `NonRetryableKind`, `RetryableKind`, `AttemptId`, `Message`, `MessageContent`, `MessageRole`, `SubmitOutcome`, `SubmitRequest`, `UsageCategories` are already imported at the top of `xai_api.rs` (lines 25-29) and thus visible in `mod tests` via `use super::*;`.

**Non-ASCII byte-splitting**: build the fixture with `serde_json::json!` (already imported at file top) so escaping is correct, e.g. a delta containing `"日本語é🙂"`. Once you have the full SSE frame as a `String`, get its raw bytes with `.into_bytes()` (or `.as_bytes()`), then split at an arbitrary byte index — the actual code under test (`xai_api.rs:555-570`) explicitly buffers partial lines across `response.chunk()` calls and only calls `std::str::from_utf8` once a full line (up to `\n`) has been reassembled, so it is safe to split a multi-byte UTF-8 character's bytes across the boundary. Example construction:

```rust
let text = "日本語é🙂";
let frame = format!(
    r#"{{"id":"req_u","model":"grok-4","choices":[{{"index":0,"delta":{{"content":{}}}}}]}}"#,
    serde_json::to_string(text).unwrap() // safely JSON-escapes/quotes the UTF-8 string
);
let full_body = sse_body(&[&frame, "[DONE]"]);
let bytes = full_body.into_bytes();
let split_at = /* choose an index that lands inside a multi-byte char, e.g. */
    bytes.iter().position(|&b| b == 0xE6 /* first byte of 日 */).unwrap() + 1;
let (first_chunk, second_chunk) = bytes.split_at(split_at);
```

## 5. Confirmed: wiremock in this codebase cannot force multi-chunk wire delivery

Confirmed — `set_body_raw` (and all `ResponseTemplate` body setters used here) produce one buffered `Full<Bytes>` hyper body; wiremock 0.6.5 gives no API in this codebase for controlling how/when that buffer is split into transport-level chunks, and there is no custom `Respond` impl or delay-based chunking helper anywhere in the repo (`grep -rn "impl Respond" .` → no hits; `grep -rn "delay(" ` on wiremock templates → no hits).

The codebase's own established workaround, already in production test code, is a **raw `tokio::net::TcpListener`-based mock server** that writes the HTTP response by hand with separate `write_all`/`flush` calls — see `crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/openai_api.rs:933-963` (`truncated_body_after_status_is_ambiguous_never_a_free_retry`), whose own comment states the reason: *"a raw server (not wiremock, whose `Full<Bytes>` responses cannot express an in-flight cutoff)"*. To force delivery of a body in two distinct chunks split at an exact byte offset, follow that same pattern: bind `TcpListener::bind("127.0.0.1:0")`, spawn a task that reads/drains the request, writes valid `HTTP/1.1 200 OK` headers (with correct `Content-Length` or `Transfer-Encoding: chunked`), then issues `socket.write_all(first_chunk).await; socket.flush().await;` followed by a second `socket.write_all(second_chunk).await; socket.flush().await;` (a short `tokio::time::sleep`/yield between the two writes can help guarantee the client observes them as separate `response.chunk()` reads rather than being coalesced by the kernel, though on loopback even back-to-back flushed writes are almost always delivered as separate reads). Then build the `XaiAdapter` with `base_url: format!("http://{addr}")` directly (bypassing `MockServer`/`adapter_for`, mirroring how the openai test constructs `OpenAiResponsesClient::new(...)` directly instead of using the wiremock helper).
That background research (launched earlier to inform test design) has now confirmed the approach I already took was correct: wiremock in this codebase can't force a response to be delivered as two distinct wire chunks (`set_body_raw` buffers the whole body), and the codebase's own precedent for true chunk-boundary control is a hand-rolled `TcpListener` mock (used in `openai_api.rs` for a similar reason). That's why I tested the fix by unit-testing the extracted `drain_complete_lines` helper directly with byte buffers split mid-codepoint, rather than trying to force it through wiremock — it's deterministic and exercises the exact code path that was broken.

No further action needed — the remediation (buffer raw bytes, decode only complete lines, plus two regression tests proving byte-identical reassembly of a non-ASCII text delta and non-ASCII tool-call arguments split mid-codepoint) is already complete and verified per my last report.
